  "name": "BILE ACIDS TOTAL - SERUM",
  "code": "1042",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"159\" data-end=\"502\">The <strong data-start=\"163\" data-end=\"194\">serum total bile acids test</strong> measures the concentration of bile acids in the blood. Bile acids are synthesized in the liver and play a key role in <strong data-start=\"313\" data-end=\"345\">fat digestion and absorption</strong>. Normally, they are <strong data-start=\"366\" data-end=\"397\">reabsorbed in the intestine</strong> and recirculated through the liver (enterohepatic circulation), with minimal amounts in the bloodstream.</p>\n<p data-start=\"504\" data-end=\"536\">This test is used to evaluate:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"537\" data-end=\"845\">\n<li data-start=\"537\" data-end=\"612\">\n<p data-start=\"539\" data-end=\"612\"><strong data-start=\"539\" data-end=\"557\">Liver function</strong>, especially early or subclinical hepatic dysfunction</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"613\" data-end=\"664\">\n<p data-start=\"615\" data-end=\"664\"><strong data-start=\"615\" data-end=\"662\">Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"665\" data-end=\"719\">\n<p data-start=\"667\" data-end=\"719\"><strong data-start=\"667\" data-end=\"717\">Biliary obstruction or hepatocellular diseases</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"720\" data-end=\"776\">\n<p data-start=\"722\" data-end=\"776\"><strong data-start=\"722\" data-end=\"750\">Metabolic liver diseases</strong> in infants and children</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"777\" data-end=\"845\">\n<p data-start=\"779\" data-end=\"845\"><strong data-start=\"779\" data-end=\"811\">Postprandial liver clearance</strong> in dynamic liver function testing</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>Elevated Bile Acids (Clinically Significant)</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy (ICP)</strong></td>\n<td>Most sensitive marker; may precede abnormal LFTs; associated with fetal distress risks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hepatitis or cirrhosis</strong></td>\n<td>Impaired bile acid uptake and clearance by damaged hepatocytes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Biliary obstruction (intra/extrahepatic)</strong></td>\n<td>Bile acids accumulate due to impaired excretion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Genetic/metabolic liver disorders</strong></td>\n<td>Seen in inborn errors of bile acid synthesis or metabolism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Portosystemic shunting (congenital or acquired)</strong></td>\n<td>Bile acids bypass liver metabolism and accumulate in circulation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
